n lean because actual presidential results here have consistently been far more Democratic than the lean implies.", "watch": "Only a historic national collapse in Democratic support or a major third-party candidacy could meaningfully narrow the margin; a Republican win is not a realistic scenario.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "The District of Columbia is the most Democratic-leaning jurisdiction in American presidential politics, with a partisan lean of D+44 and recent presidential results in which the Democratic nominee has taken roughly nine in ten votes. No incumbent is on the ballot in 2028, but no Republican has ever come close here, and the Republican share of the vote has remained in the single digits across recent cycles.\n\nNo public polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this race, and none are really needed: the fundamentals alone point to an overwhelming Democratic win. Fundraising and outside spending data are likewise absent, reflecting how little contestable ground exists. The forecast expects a Democratic margin of roughly 52 points, with the realistic uncertainty concerning the size of the Democratic victory rather than which party prevails.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9995, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9999, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9999, "margin": 52.0}]}